WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.340
Back in 2019 some AI researchers and&nbsp;
mathematicians came up with an ambitious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:11.220
challenge, to create an AI that could win a&nbsp;
gold medal at the International Mathematics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:17.400
Olympiad. It was an ambitious challenge because&nbsp;
winning a gold medal at this event puts you on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:22.680
the world stage as having one of the best&nbsp;
mathematical minds. Previous winners of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:28.440
gold medal include Terence Tao and Maryan&nbsp;
Mirzakhani. The researchers called it the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:33.900
IMO Grand Challenge and for an AI system&nbsp;
to pass they proposed the following rules.

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:39.060
Each proof that the AI producers must&nbsp;
be checkable in 10 minutes which is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:43.860
approximately the amount of time it takes&nbsp;
a human judge to judge a human solution.

00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:49.440
The AI only has as much time as&nbsp;
a human competitor which is four&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:55.140
and a half hours for each set of three&nbsp;
problems and the AI must be open source&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.140 --> 00:01:00.300
released publicly and reproducible.&nbsp;
The AI cannot query the internet.

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:07.620
As of recording this we have met chat GPT and&nbsp;
GPT-4 was released a couple of months ago. I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:16.020
sure that we'll be continually amazed by what it's&nbsp;
able to do but no AI including ChatGPT has yet won&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:23.400
or even competed in the IMO competition. Bets are&nbsp;
on literally for if and when one might succeed.

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:31.740
GPT-4 is paraded around as having already aced&nbsp;
many other exams including the SAT and the biology&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:39.840
Olympiad but it might never ace the IMO because&nbsp;
the thing is that ChatGPT is actually not very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:46.860
good at math. It's not good at counting or keeping&nbsp;
track of multiple operations because after all it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:52.740
is a language model that excels at predicting&nbsp;
the next word in a sentence. Math questions on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:59.460
the SAT can be quite predictable and formulaic&nbsp;
and likely enough similar problems were included&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:06.360
in the training data set that it was able to&nbsp;
get by but the IMO problems are different and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:12.180
they're designed to test true understanding and&nbsp;
creative problem solving. I'd like to understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:18.720
the likelihood of an AI passing this challenge&nbsp;
soon but in order to do that I would first need to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:26.160
understand the solution to an IMO problem myself&nbsp;
on human terms then I would like to understand how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:33.240
ChatGPT goes about solving it and then see if&nbsp;
there are any better AI competitors out there.

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:39.480
Here's an IMO problem. This is the Olympics for&nbsp;
math so these problems are some of the hardest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:45.360
out there and can require creative solutions.&nbsp;
This is problem six from the 2022 competition:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:53.400
Let n be a positive integer. A Nordic square is an&nbsp;
n times n board containing all the integers from 1&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.400 --> 00:03:00.060
to n squared so that each cell contains exactly&nbsp;
one number. Two different cells are considered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:06.780
adjacent if they share a common side. Every cell&nbsp;
that is adjacent only to cells containing larger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.780 --> 00:03:14.340
numbers is called a valley. An uphill path is&nbsp;
a sequence of one or more cells such that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:20.160
first cell in the sequence is a valley. Each&nbsp;
subsequent cell in the sequence is adjacent to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:25.500
the previous cell and the numbers written in the&nbsp;
cells in the sequence are an increasing order.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:32.040
Find as a function of n the smallest possible&nbsp;
number of uphill paths in a Nordic Square.

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:38.580
This is an example of a Nordic Square where n&nbsp;
is equal to two. Here one is our valley and we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.580 --> 00:03:45.360
could have six possible uphill paths; one to&nbsp;
two, then one to two to four which is counted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:52.380
as a different uphill path, you could do one to&nbsp;
three or one to three to four and we also have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.380 --> 00:03:57.600
include the path of length one which is just the&nbsp;
valley. We could have placed these numbers in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:02.520
squares in any the order it didn't have to be in&nbsp;
this pattern it could have gone one, two, three,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:07.980
four, and in fact you should play around with it&nbsp;
now trying Nordic squares of different sizes and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:14.400
different arrangements of the numbers to see what&nbsp;
kinds of patterns might give you this smallest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:20.340
number of total uphill paths. If you want to try&nbsp;
it for yourself now is your time to pause. I don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.340 --> 00:04:25.740
think it's possible for a human to look at this&nbsp;
and know the solution instantly you really do have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.740 --> 00:04:31.740
to play around and try a bunch of random things to&nbsp;
see if any patterns pop out. Here's an example of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.740 --> 00:04:36.960
a three by three Nordic Square which looks like&nbsp;
it might have the minimum paths for the size.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:45.900
Again we only have one Valley and it is our one&nbsp;
but we could also have one two, one two six, one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.900 --> 00:04:57.180
two seven, one three, one three eight, one three&nbsp;
seven, one five, one five nine, one five six,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.960 --> 00:05:06.240
one four, one four eight, one four nine, giving a&nbsp;
total of thirteen path. You can easily find other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:11.880
three by three arrangements that have more paths&nbsp;
than this and the crux of the solution comes down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:19.380
from recognizing two things; one is that for the&nbsp;
minimum number of paths you want there to be only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.380 --> 00:05:26.520
one valley, like we have here our number one. And&nbsp;
you also need to recognize that for every pair of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:34.200
adjacent numbers there needs to be only one path&nbsp;
back to the valley. Say here two and six are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:41.280
adjacent numbers and from them there was only one&nbsp;
path back down to our valley which was like this.

00:05:41.280 --> 00:05:46.920
This essentially means you can have two&nbsp;
paths coming to a common endpoint but you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.920 --> 00:05:54.060
can never have this two paths converging&nbsp;
and then continuing on because that would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.060 --> 00:05:59.340
mean that from wherever this ends these&nbsp;
two adjacent numbers will have multiple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.340 --> 00:06:05.220
paths back down to the valley so this is not&nbsp;
allowed and we can call something like this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.220 --> 00:06:10.140
our end point, a sink. With this we've&nbsp;
given ourselves the golden ticket,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.140 --> 00:06:17.460
if every two adjacent numbers only have one path&nbsp;
back to the valley then the minimum number of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.460 --> 00:06:25.020
paths in our Nordic Square would be equal to the&nbsp;
number of adjacent numbers. Counting the pairs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.020 --> 00:06:32.820
of adjacent numbers here we have one, two, in&nbsp;
each row and there's three rows so that would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.900 --> 00:06:40.860
two times three, that's six adjacent&nbsp;
numbers that are adjacent horizontally. Plus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.460 --> 00:06:52.440
one, two, three, four, five, six, going vertically&nbsp;
so there'd be 12 pairs of adjacent numbers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.440 --> 00:07:00.240
That's our number of minimum paths but we have to&nbsp;
remember to add one because our valley, our single&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.240 --> 00:07:08.640
valley will also be its own single length path&nbsp;
giving 13 as the total minimum paths in this case.

00:07:08.640 --> 00:07:17.520
To extend that for any number of n we will always&nbsp;
have n minus 1 adjacent numbers on each row times&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:24.480
n rows and n minus 1 adjacent numbers on each&nbsp;
column times the number of columns so that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.140 --> 00:07:34.860
2 n times n minus 1 paths plus one to account for&nbsp;
our valley. That's basically the answer here and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.860 --> 00:07:41.160
that will give you some points but more points&nbsp;
will come from new harder task of describing how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.160 --> 00:07:46.680
to arrange the numbers to get this minimum and&nbsp;
that part of the solution comes from describing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.680 --> 00:07:53.340
a tree-like structure like this. If you put the&nbsp;
numbers in increasing order in these trees you end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.340 --> 00:08:00.540
up with only one Valley and for every adjacent&nbsp;
pair of numbers only one path back to it. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.540 --> 00:08:06.600
black numbers here represent numbers higher&nbsp;
than 153 so you fill in all these ones first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.600 --> 00:08:12.000
and then go back and fill in the black spaces&nbsp;
with the higher ones. These black numbers are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:17.700
sinks and there might be multiple ways to end&nbsp;
at the sink but there'll never be a way out.

00:08:17.700 --> 00:08:24.120
Let's give the problem to ChatGPT and see what&nbsp;
it does. The 2022 problem I picked wouldn't be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.120 --> 00:08:32.640
in the GPT training data set. I'm using the latest&nbsp;
model GPT-4 and it gives me the wrong answer. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:39.300
says that the smallest number of paths is equal to&nbsp;
n. I asked it how many valleys there should be to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.300 --> 00:08:46.440
minimize the number of paths and it immediately&nbsp;
gives a square with three valleys. It says that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.440 --> 00:08:54.240
the minimum is n valleys which gives n paths and&nbsp;
if you remember recognizing that there must only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.240 --> 00:09:01.560
be one valley is key to getting the solution. I&nbsp;
asked it to make a Nordic square with only one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.560 --> 00:09:08.940
valley which it did but then it failed to count&nbsp;
more than one path and when I asked it to do it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.940 --> 00:09:16.020
for larger squares it gave constructions that for&nbsp;
each adjacent pair there were multiple paths back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.020 --> 00:09:21.720
to the valley and it just couldn't count&nbsp;
paths correctly even when I tried to point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.720 --> 00:09:26.760
out mistakes. I don't think it would be able to&nbsp;
score any points on this question at the moment.

00:09:26.760 --> 00:09:33.420
This recent paper from Microsoft analyzed the&nbsp;
abilities of GPT-4 and said there were sparks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.420 --> 00:09:38.940
of artificial general intelligence which&nbsp;
refers to AI that is broadly intelligent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.940 --> 00:09:44.940
at or above human level and not just good at&nbsp;
one specific task. They mentioned that ChatGPT&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.540 --> 00:09:52.080
is trained on a massive amount of webtext&nbsp;
data and uses at its core a self-supervised&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.080 --> 00:09:57.660
objective of predicting the next word in a&nbsp;
partial sentence. The paper also shows that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.660 --> 00:10:04.620
GPT-4 does not have the capacity required to&nbsp;
conduct mathematical research. It does manage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.620 --> 00:10:10.620
to solve one IMO question in here but in many&nbsp;
other problems it lacks critical reasoning and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.620 --> 00:10:15.540
the ability to examine each step of its&nbsp;
arguments. They say one reason for this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.540 --> 00:10:21.120
might be that in most of the math solutions&nbsp;
it's read on the internet, most just give the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.120 --> 00:10:27.720
correct answer but don't capture the thinking&nbsp;
process. It doesn't make guesses or backtrack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.720 --> 00:10:34.260
because it can't backtrack. When predicting&nbsp;
the next word it just needs to move forward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.260 --> 00:10:40.860
in a straight line. In essence it's bad at math&nbsp;
because it lacks the ability to play around.

00:10:41.640 --> 00:10:48.360
So ChatGPT fails for now but it's not the&nbsp;
only competitor. The people at OpenAI,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.360 --> 00:10:54.000
in fact some of the same people who came up with&nbsp;
the IMO Grand Challenge in the first place, work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.000 --> 00:11:01.440
on a different AI system. One that according to&nbsp;
this paper published in 2022 has already managed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.440 --> 00:11:09.360
to solve some IMO problems. It's not a language&nbsp;
model but instead is a proof-solving model that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.360 --> 00:11:15.120
speaks the language of formal math. Automated&nbsp;
proof solvers have existed for a little while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.120 --> 00:11:22.260
but this one is trained to iteratively search for&nbsp;
new proofs. It takes mathematical ideas that need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.260 --> 00:11:28.860
to be proved and breaks them down into smaller and&nbsp;
smaller statements that are easier to prove until&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.860 --> 00:11:34.500
there's nothing left to prove. This is what the&nbsp;
formal math language looks like, it's not too easy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.500 --> 00:11:40.560
for a human to read but it's probably our best&nbsp;
bet when creating a system that is actually useful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.560 --> 00:11:46.560
to mathematicians. It uses something called the&nbsp;
lean theorem prover. The proofs that it creates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.560 --> 00:11:53.160
are machine checkable, following a series of&nbsp;
logical laws. They say that their model and search&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.160 --> 00:12:00.300
procedure are capable of producing proofs that&nbsp;
chain multiple non-trivial reasoning steps and it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.300 --> 00:12:07.200
does require some creativity to invent lines like&nbsp;
this. Combining an AI that can speak formal math&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.200 --> 00:12:14.580
and correctly prove statements with something like&nbsp;
ChatGPT to make it more user-friendly could really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.580 --> 00:12:20.040
make some waves in math and much more likely&nbsp;
to pass the IMO Grand Challenge than a language&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.040 --> 00:12:27.540
model alone. And as I mentioned while ChatGPT is&nbsp;
still struggling with IMO problems it has already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.540 --> 00:12:34.560
passed a bunch of exams. There's a note here back&nbsp;
in the Microsoft paper saying that GPT-4 does not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.560 --> 00:12:41.040
rely on memorizing the exact problem statement&nbsp;
but on applying a general solution method. While&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.040 --> 00:12:48.600
it is possible that GPT-4 memorizes the solution&nbsp;
template this is not necessarily a flaw as it is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.600 --> 00:12:55.260
also a common way of solving math problems for&nbsp;
humans. They're saying that GPT-4 has been able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.260 --> 00:13:02.700
to pass a bunch of exams by doing what many of us&nbsp;
do which is kind of memorizing the structure of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.700 --> 00:13:09.420
common problems. Looking through past papers and&nbsp;
solutions can often give you enough of what you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.420 --> 00:13:15.720
need to do well in an exam because the structures&nbsp;
are usually pretty much the same and if this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.720 --> 00:13:22.020
what ChatGPT is so good at too then perhaps&nbsp;
this is the space in which exams will change&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.020 --> 00:13:29.040
a bit. Perhaps for now exams will have to change&nbsp;
to be a little bit more like the IMO to reward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.040 --> 00:13:36.300
creative problem solving and necessitate having&nbsp;
to stop and play around with the problem. That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.300 --> 00:13:43.320
seems like it is still a uniquely human trait but&nbsp;
I'm not sure for how long that will be the case.

00:13:43.320 --> 00:13:47.160
Thanks for watching this video and&nbsp;
thanks to my Patreon supporters for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.160 --> 00:13:52.500
making it possible. A special shout out to&nbsp;
today's Patron Cat of the Day, Cathulhu.

00:13:53.880 --> 00:13:54.540
[Music]

